# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: your parents split up (pairs D1b)

== your parents split up
tier: life event
stages: child juvenile young_adult
age: 12-18
per_year: .02 .02 .01 0 0 0
drivers: stress+.4 money-.3 community-.3 era+.2
once: yes
times: once, sometimes twice: about 2.5 in 100 children and 2 in 100 teenagers a year see their parents part, and 1 in 100 young adults still at home (D46, estimate); more where parents never married, fewer in much of Europe. Point 12 (Emren 2026-10-05): once: parents part once; a second split (a step-parent) is rare and not told as this event.
likelier: money worries, family strife, a parent's drinking or addiction, very young or unmarried parents, a time when divorce is easy and common
rarer: enough money, a faith or a close community, a close and steady family, parents who married older
tone: trouble
life: family
horizon: years
roles: parent, sibling, grandparent
earth: your parents tell you they are going to live in two houses
scene: {Ns} parents sit {N} down at the kitchen table, both with careful faces. They are separating, and it sounds as if they rehearsed the words.
scene W: {N} wants to know how it will work: which nights where, who pays for what, where the holidays go. If there is a plan, it can be lived with.
scene U: {N} has heard the arguments through the floor for a year and has a fair idea of the reasons already. Now {N} wants the true version.
scene B: Two houses means two sets of rules, and two parents who both feel guilty. {N} notices how much room that leaves.
scene R: {N} is too old to cry in front of them and too angry not to. The chair scrapes back hard across the floor.
scene G: That evening {grandparent} rings. Some things change, {grandparent} says, but a family is still a family.
- W | keep to the agreed week between the two homes, and keep up at school | v: security, conformity | chance: 78
- U | ask each parent, on their own, for the real reasons, and compare the answers | v: self-direction | chance: 50
- B | play your parents off against each other for money, freedom and a later curfew | habit | v: power, hedonism | chance: 65
- R | tell them both, tears and all, how much this hurts and that you love them | v: none clear (feelings spoken straight out) | chance: 65
- G | move in with your grandparents for a while, away from both houses | v: security, tradition | closed: approval: breaking the agreed week; backfire: a row between the grown-ups, and the week enforced | chance: 65
- RG | refuse to change schools or leave your friends, whatever the grown-ups decide | identity | v: security, tradition | chance: 75
- UB | listen in on their phone calls, to know what is coming before anyone says | habit | v: security | closed: approval: eavesdropping; backfire: overhearing far more than is bearable | chance: 75
- UR | choose which parent to live with, and argue your case to both | identity | v: self-direction | chance: 70
- WB | get both parents to agree one set of rules, and hold them to it | v: security, power | chance: 45
- WG | keep the peace at both houses, and keep how you feel to yourself | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 45
works: Slowly the two houses become two homes, and {N} has a toothbrush in each.
works: {Ns} parents are kinder to each other apart than they were together, and {N} can feel the difference.
fails: {N} goes quiet at school, and the teacher calls home.
fails: The Friday handovers are cold and tense, and {N} dreads every one.

