# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a pet of your own (cycle s1)

== a pet of your own
tier: life event
stages: child juvenile
age: 4-16
per_year: .1 .07 0 0 0 0
drivers: money+.4 health+.2 era+.2
once: yes
times: about 10 in 100 children and 7 in 100 teenagers a year see a new animal join the family (D42, estimate); about two thirds of US households keep a pet, and pets live 10 to 15 years. Point 12 (Emren 2026-10-05): once: the first animal of one's own; later pets come with the [dog of one's own] and [cat of one's own] perks (point 12: two in 15 of 100 test lives came within two years).
likelier: an owned house with a garden, money enough, children at home, a parent who grew up with animals, after a pet has died
rarer: renting, a landlord who says no, frequent moves, money trouble, poor health or allergies
tone: joy
life: home, nature
horizon: years
roles: parent, sibling, a neighbour, a vet
earth: a kitten of your own, and the job of looking after it
scene: A kitten tumbles out of a cardboard box onto the kitchen floor. {parent} says it belongs to {N}, and so does looking after it.
scene W: {parent} has written a list on the fridge: feed twice a day, fresh water, clean the tray. {N} reads it and nods, very seriously.
scene U: {N} has already borrowed three library books about cats, and wants to know what every twitch of that tail means.
scene B: It is {Ns} kitten, not {Ns} sibling's, and {N} intends to make that clear from the very first day.
scene R: The kitten pounces on {Ns} shoelace, and {N} is instantly and completely in love.
scene G: The kitten finds the warmest patch of sun on the floor and curls up in it. {N} lies down beside it.
- W | feed it and clean up after it every day, exactly as you promised | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 55
- U | read every library book about cats, and learn what its tail is saying | v: self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 90
- B | keep it in your room, and let your sibling hold it only when you say | v: power | chance: 73
- R | play with it for hours, chasing string all over the house | habit | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 92
- G | let it sleep on your bed every night, curled against your feet | v: benevolence, universalism (nature) | closed: approval: no pets on the beds; backfire: fleas, and the bedding stripped | chance: 75
- W>U | ask the vet how to look after it properly, and write it all down | door | v: self-direction, conformity | chance: 70
- U>B | teach it tricks so it wins the pet show at the school fair | identity | v: achievement, power | chance: 20
- B>R | skip your chores to play with the kitten, and say you were feeding it | v: hedonism | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: lying; backfire: found out, and kitten time rationed | chance: 70
- R>G | carry it out into the garden to show it the grass and the birds | v: universalism (nature), stimulation | chance: 80
- G>W | introduce it slowly to the neighbour's old dog, until the two can share the garden | v: benevolence, security | mark: made a friend | chance: 50
